package build.terminal

import mill.*
import mill.javalib.DepSyntax

object `package` extends build.TuiPublishModule {

  def artifactName = "tui-terminal"

  def moduleDescription = "JLine terminal backend for the tui library."

  def moduleDeps = Seq(build.core)

  def mvnDeps = super.mvnDeps() ++ Seq(
    // JLine 4.x, not the org.jline:jline bundle. That bundle is one shaded jar of 773 entries and 1.65 MB — the
    // line reader, a built-in SSH server, a telnet server, a shell (new in 4.x), and every terminal provider —
    // while this module imports exactly four types from it: `org.jline.terminal.{Attributes, Terminal,
    // TerminalBuilder}` and `org.jline.utils.InfoCmp`. (A few more of the bundle's types are reached without being
    // imported — `Terminal.Signal`, `Terminal.SignalHandler`, `InfoCmp.Capability`, the inferred `Terminal.Size`
    // and `NonBlockingReader` — all of them still present and source-compatible across the 3.x -> 4.x jump.)
    // glyphora draws its own frames and reads raw bytes; it never asks JLine to read a line, so none of the rest is
    // ever called.
    //
    // `jline-terminal` is the API and its pure-Java parts; `jline-terminal-jni` is the provider that actually opens
    // the TTY, and both depend on `jline-native` (4.x split the JNI glue out of `jline-terminal-jni` itself).
    // Together that is 332 entries and 0.66 MB. Every consumer of tui-terminal inherits the smaller graph, and a
    // native image stops having to reason about an SSH server: `examples.hello-world.nativeImage` is 22.37 MB
    // total image size / 21.11 MB on disk under this JLine version and GraalVM 25.0.2 together (re-measured after
    // this bump; the reachable-type count from the pre-bump figure was not re-captured in the same pass).
    //
    // Signal handling changed underneath `JLine3Backend.enterRawMode`/`JLine3Backend.onStop` in this jump: JLine
    // 4.1.3 clears ISIG when entering raw mode (so the kernel no longer turns Ctrl+C/Ctrl+Z into SIGINT/SIGTSTP on
    // its own), and 4.1.4's compensating `org.jline.terminal.softwareSignals` layer raises the JLine-level signal
    // *and* still passes the control byte through to the reader — its own upstream Scaladoc warns the default may
    // flip in a future release. `JLine3Backend` pins it to `false` explicitly (see `create`) and keeps synthesizing
    // the interrupt from the control byte the way `InputDecoder` already did before this bump, rather than
    // inheriting a default JLine itself says is not stable.
    //
    // JEP 472 (JDK 24+): `jline-native`'s JNI loader would otherwise print four `WARNING:` lines to stderr on
    // every run — confirmed on the built `examples.hello-world.nativeImage` binary under GraalVM 25.0.2/JDK 25.
    // The binary still exits 1 with the expected message (CI's check is a substring match, not exact), so the
    // noise was the only symptom. `TuiExampleModule.nativeImageOptions` (build.mill) passes
    // `--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED` to every native image, which silences it.
    mvn"org.jline:jline-terminal:4.4.5",
    mvn"org.jline:jline-terminal-jni:4.4.5",
  )

  object test extends TuiSupportTests
}
